---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import {
  renderSaleBreakdownSvg,
  saleBreakdownBars,
  chartAltText,
  money,
  PARTS,
  CHART_PNG_PATH,
  CHART_TITLE,
} from "../lib/sale-breakdown-chart";

const site = "https://marginready.com";
const path = "/how-to-sell-on-tiktok-shop/";
const url = site + path;
const title = "How to Sell on TikTok Shop for Beginners: Setup to Profit";
const description =
  "What TikTok asks for at sign-up, the order of the setup steps, and the math to run before you list: what one sale leaves after the fee, cost and shipping.";

// TikTok sources, re-read 2026-11-23.
// Registration guide (rendered page; requirements, seller types, sign-up methods).
const REGISTER_URL = "https://seller.tiktok.com/how-to-register";
// Seller registration requirements (ads.tiktok.com help, last updated August 2025).
const REQUIREMENTS_URL =
  "https://ads.tiktok.com/help/article/requirements-to-register-as-a-seller-on-tiktok-shop";
// US referral fee: 6% per order from April 1, 2024.
const REFERRAL_FEE_URL =
  "https://seller-us.tiktok.com/university/essay?knowledge_id=5982454398175018&lang=en";
// Referral fee by category: most at 6%, some jewelry and pre-owned at 5%.
const REFERRAL_BY_CATEGORY_URL =
  "https://seller-us.tiktok.com/university/essay?knowledge_id=5988482086864682&lang=en";

const articleLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "How to start selling on TikTok Shop, and make money on your first product",
  url,
  description,
  publisher: { "@id": "https://marginready.com/#organization" },
  isPartOf: { "@id": "https://marginready.com/#website" },
};

const faq = [
  {
    q: "How much does it cost to start selling on TikTok Shop?",
    a: "TikTok's registration guide lists documents and details to provide, not a sign-up fee. What TikTok takes is a referral fee on each order: 6% in most US categories, 5% in a few. Your real startup cost is your own: the first batch of inventory, samples, packaging and shipping.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need an LLC or a registered business?",
    a: "No. TikTok has an individual seller type for people selling under their own name, alongside sole proprietorship and corporation or partnership. As an individual you give an SSN or ITIN; a registered business gives a TIN or EIN.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use my existing TikTok account to sign up?",
    a: "Yes. TikTok lets you create the seller account with an existing TikTok account, by QR code or login, or with a phone number or email that isn't already tied to another TikTok Shop.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need followers to sell on TikTok Shop?",
    a: "TikTok's seller registration guide lists no follower count. It asks for age, residency, ID, tax and bank details. Followers help a product get seen, but they are not a condition for opening a shop.",
  },
  {
    q: "I already earn commission as a creator. What changes when I sell my own product?",
    a: "You stop being paid a share of someone else's sale and start paying the costs yourself: the referral fee, the product, shipping, returns, and commission to any other creator who promotes it. Your own videos don't cost you a commission, which is the advantage. Run the numbers per product before you order stock.",
  },
];

const faqLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};

const link = "underline hover:text-foreground";

// v1.N chart: one SVG from calcFees, inline here and as the committed PNG.
const chartSvg = renderSaleBreakdownSvg();
const chartBars = saleBreakdownBars();
const chartAlt = chartAltText();
const pngUrl = site + CHART_PNG_PATH;
const embedCode =
  `<a href="${url}"><img src="${pngUrl}" alt="${chartAlt.replace(/"/g, "&quot;")}" width="1200" height="630" /></a>\n` +
  `<p>Chart: <a href="${url}">${CHART_TITLE}, MarginReady</a></p>`;
---

<Layout>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="canonical" href={url} />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />

    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content={url} />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="MarginReady" />
    <meta property="og:image" content={pngUrl} />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content={chartAlt} />

    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={pngUrl} />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={title} />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />

    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(articleLd)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)} />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10 sm:py-14">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How to start selling on TikTok Shop, and make money on your first product
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
        To sell on TikTok Shop in the US you register in Seller Center, verify
        your identity (you must be 18 or older and a US resident, with a
        government ID, an SSN, ITIN or EIN, a US address and a bank account), and
        then list products. TikTok's guide asks for documents, not a sign-up fee. The step most
        beginners skip comes next: working out what one sale actually leaves you
        after TikTok's 6% referral fee, your product cost and shipping, before
        you order stock or pay a creator.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-tk space-y-12">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          What TikTok asks for before you can sell
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
          Per TikTok's
          <a class={link} href={REGISTER_URL} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
            >step-by-step seller guide</a
          >, every US seller must be at least 18, be a US resident, provide a
          valid government-issued ID (passport, driver's license, state ID or
          permanent resident card) and provide a US business address. On top of
          that you'll enter:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Identity details:</strong> full legal
            name, date of birth and your ID's expiration date. They must match the
            ID you upload.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Tax number:</strong> an SSN or ITIN if
            you sell as an individual, or a TIN or EIN for a registered business.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Contact details:</strong> a phone
            number and email that
            <a class={link} href={REQUIREMENTS_URL} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
              >aren't already used for another TikTok Shop</a
            >, and a residential or registered business address.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">A bank account</strong> to receive
            payouts.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
          You also pick a seller type: <strong class="text-foreground">individual</strong>
          (selling under your own name), <strong class="text-foreground">sole proprietorship</strong>
          (an unincorporated business owned by one person) or
          <strong class="text-foreground">corporation or partnership</strong>. If
          you're testing one product, individual is the type built for you; you
          don't need to form a company first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          Setting up the account, step by step
        </h2>
        <ol class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5 text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Create the seller account</strong> in
            TikTok Shop Seller Center. You can sign in with an existing TikTok
            account (QR code or login) or register with a phone number or email.
            TikTok sends a code by SMS or email to confirm it, then you set a
            password.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Choose your seller type and verify.</strong>
            Upload your ID as a clear PDF, JPEG or PNG under 10MB. Every detail
            has to match the document; when it doesn't, TikTok asks you to correct
            it or upload again.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Add tax, contact and bank details.</strong>
            A green checkmark shows when verification passes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">List your first product.</strong>
            TikTok's guide says you can list and sell once verification is
            complete. Before you do, run the numbers in the next section: the
            price you list at decides whether every later sale makes or loses
            money.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
          If you're a creator, step 1 is where your two roles meet: you can sign
          up with the TikTok account you already post from instead of starting a
          new one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          The part setup guides skip: what one sale leaves you
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
          A sale on TikTok Shop is not your profit. Out of every order comes the
          <a class={link} href={REFERRAL_FEE_URL} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
            >6% referral fee</a
          > (5% in a few
          <a class={link} href={REFERRAL_BY_CATEGORY_URL} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
            >jewelry and pre-owned categories</a
          >), then your own costs: what the product cost you landed at your door,
          and shipping it to the buyer. If a creator sold it, their commission
          comes out too, and so does any ad spend. What's left is your profit:
        </p>
        <p
          class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/40 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-foreground tabular-nums"
        >
          profit per sale = price &minus; referral fee &minus; product cost
          &minus; shipping &minus; creator commission &minus; ad cost &minus;
          returns
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
          Beginners usually get two inputs wrong. The product cost is not the
          supplier's unit price: freight, duties, packaging and the units that
          arrive damaged all belong in it, and the
          <a class={link} href="/how-to-calculate-cogs-tiktok-shop/">landed COGS guide</a>
          works it out from one purchase order. And shipping is a cost per
          order you pay, even when the buyer sees "free shipping".
        </p>
        <figure class="mt-6">
          <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border [&>svg]:block [&>svg]:h-auto [&>svg]:w-full" set:html={chartSvg} />
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            The same $25 product sold two ways. Selling through a creator with
            ads adds two costs, and what you keep drops from
            {money(chartBars[0].kept)} to {money(chartBars[1].kept)} per order.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <details class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer">Show the chart as a table</summary>
          <table class="mt-3 w-full text-left tabular-nums">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-border">
                <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 font-semibold text-foreground">Part of the $25 sale</th>
                {chartBars.map((b) => <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 font-semibold text-foreground">{b.label}</th>)}
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                PARTS.map((p) => (
                  <tr class="border-b border-border">
                    <td class="py-1.5 pr-4">{p.name}</td>
                    {chartBars.map((b) => {
                      const seg = b.segments.find((x) => x.key === p.key);
                      return <td class="py-1.5 pr-4">{seg ? money(seg.amount) : "—"}</td>;
                    })}
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </details>
        <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/40 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          <p>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Use this chart.</strong> You're welcome to
            share it or put it in a post or guide; please link back to this page.
            <a class={link} href={CHART_PNG_PATH} download>Download the PNG</a>
            (1200 &times; 630) or copy the embed code:
          </p>
          <textarea
            readonly
            rows="4"
            aria-label="Embed code for the chart"
            class="mt-2 w-full rounded-md border border-border bg-background p-2 font-mono text-xs text-foreground"
          >{embedCode}</textarea>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          Three first products, worked through
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-6 text-muted-foreground">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-foreground">
              A $25 product you promote yourself
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              $25 price, $8 landed product cost, $5 shipping, 6% referral fee.
              The fee is $1.50, so one sale leaves $10.50, a 42% margin. Your own
              videos cost you no commission, which is why selling your own product
              from your own account is the easiest place to start.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-foreground">
              The same product, sold through creators with ads
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Add a 15% creator commission ($3.75) and $3 of ad spend per order.
              The sale now leaves $3.75, a 15% margin. Still profitable, but most
              of the room is gone, and a higher commission or a pricier ad
              would push it to zero. Find that limit first with the
              <a class={link} href="/tiktok-shop-affiliate-commission-calculator/"
                >affiliate commission calculator</a
              > and the
              <a class={link} href="/tiktok-shop-roas-calculator/">break-even ROAS calculator</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-foreground">
              A $12 product that loses money on every order
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              $12 price, $5 product cost, $5 shipping. After the $0.72 referral
              fee one sale leaves $1.28, a 10.7% margin, and that's before
              anything else. Pay a creator 10% ($1.20) and count a 5% return
              rate as lost revenue ($0.60 per order), and each sale loses $0.52.
              Selling more makes it worse. A cheap product with
              a full-size shipping cost is the most common beginner trap: raise the
              price, bundle units into one order, or pick a different product. The
              <a class={link} href="/tiktok-shop-break-even-calculator/"
                >break-even price calculator</a
              > gives the lowest price that works.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          Before your first order ships
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Price from your costs, not from competitors.</strong>
            Copying the price of a similar listing copies their margin, not
            yours. Check your price in the
            <a class={link} href="/tiktok-shop-fee-calculator/">fee and profit calculator</a>
            with your own landed cost and shipping.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Decide on creator commission before you open it to creators.</strong>
            A rate that sounds normal can be more than a cheap product can carry,
            as the $12 example shows.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Plan cash for the wait.</strong>
            You pay for stock and shipping up front, and the money from a sale
            arrives later. The
            <a class={link} href="/tiktok-shop-payout-schedule/">payout schedule guide</a>
            explains when, so you can restock without running dry.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Expect the payout to be smaller than your sales.</strong>
            When it is, the
            <a class={link} href="/why-tiktok-shop-payout-is-less-than-sales/"
              >GMV vs settlement guide</a
            > walks through every deduction, so a normal gap doesn't look like a
            missing payment.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          All of these are free and run in your browser; the
          <a class={link} href="/tools/">TikTok Shop seller tools</a> page puts
          them in the order to use them. Once you're selling,
          <a class={link} href="/">MarginReady</a> will read your settled
          payouts and show the real profit on each product, so you're not
          estimating any more.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          Questions new sellers ask
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
          {
            faq.map((f) => (
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-foreground">{f.q}</h3>
                <p class="mt-1.5 text-muted-foreground">{f.a}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
